        <h2 style="font-size: 1.5em;">6. REA with other ABB arc fault protection solutions</h2>
        <p>Figure 10 shows a switchgear arc fault protection solution based on the <span data-field="module_id_1">{{ module_id_1 }}</span> module. The actual case could represent an industrial main switchgear system or a switchgear maintenance staff. Both the arc fault protection system and the arc fault protection system performance specifications are provided in the <span data-field="document_ref_1">{{ document_ref_1 }}</span> series devices provides selective protection on arc faults in the cable termination compartment.</p>
        <p>The arc fault protection system is based on two <span data-field="module_id_1">{{ module_id_1 }}</span> modules, which are interconnected using fiber-optic links. The CURRENT messages are sent between the main modules via fiber-optic links. The arc fault protection system with two <span data-field="module_id_1">{{ module_id_1 }}</span> modules is selective. Both sections of the busbar system incorporate their own loop-type fiber sensor for arc fault detection. In this protection scheme the CBFP function is implemented by tripping the HV side circuit breaker.</p>
        <div style="text-align: center; margin-top: 30px;">
            <!-- Placeholder for Figure 10 Diagram -->
            <div style="height: 300px; border: 1px solid #ccc; display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center; margin-bottom: 20px;">
                [Diagram Placeholder for Figure 10]
            </div>
            <p class="figure-caption">Figure 10. A redundant and selective arc fault protection system of a single-busbar switchgear panel fed from two power transformers.</p>
        </div>
        <p>The <span data-field="document_ref_2">{{ document_ref_2 }}</span> device of the in feeder cubicle measures the phase currents on the transformer HV side, which makes the arc fault protection system cover the whole area from the terminals of the power transformer right to the switchgear busbars. One of the lens sensors can be installed to detect arc flashes on the busbar system, and the other lens sensors can be installed in the cable termination compartments. These relays provide independent protection of their respective cubicles on faults downstream of the main busbar system. The arc fault protection system of the in feeder cubicle is based on their own current measurement information and light detection by sensors located in the cable termination compartments. The CBFP function is implemented by tripping the HV side circuit breaker.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="page">
        <h2 style="font-size: 1.5em;">REA in combination with Ultra-Fast Earthing Switch (UFES)</h2>
        <div style="text-align: center; margin-top: 50px;">
            <!-- Placeholder for Figure 11 Diagram -->
            <div style="height: 300px; border: 1px solid #ccc; display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center; margin-bottom: 20px;">
                [Diagram Placeholder for Figure 11]
            </div>
            <p class="figure-caption">Figure 11. Fast arc fault detection and elimination in a switchgear system fed by one power transformer.</p>
        </div>
        <p>The arc fault protection system of Figure 11 incorporates arc detection and elimination hardware. In the REA arc fault protection system and an UFES have been combined. The UFES comprises a QRU100 device (Quick Release Unit) and PSE devices (Primary Switching Elements). The solution of the arc fault protection system is based on the <span data-field="module_id_1">{{ module_id_1 }}</span> module, which detects an arc fault of arc fault is utmost importance to minimize equipment damage and to ensure the safety of personnel. The <span data-field="module_id_1">{{ module_id_1 }}</span> module is activated when an arc fault is <span data-field="arc_fault_condition">{{ arc_fault_condition }}</span>. The main module <span data-field="module_id_1">{{ module_id_1 }}</span> detects fault currents. The protection function of the <span data-field="module_id_1">{{ module_id_1 }}</span> module is activated when an arc fault occurs between the phases in the busbar system. The <span data-field="module_id_1">{{ module_id_1 }}</span> module is equipped with a lens-type sensor for detecting arc faults occurring in the in feeder cubicle. The system is extended with a <span data-field="module_id_2">{{ module_id_2 }}</span> module equipped with eight lens-type sensors for detecting arc faults in the busbar cubicle. The <span data-field="module_id_1">{{ module_id_1 }}</span> module sends a LIGHT message to the <span data-field="module_id_2">{{ module_id_2 }}</span> module, which issues a trip signal when the simultaneous overcurrent condition for tripping is met.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="page">
        <div style="text-align: center; margin-top: 50px;">
            <!-- Placeholder for Figure 12 Diagram -->
            <div style="height: 350px; border: 1px solid #ccc; display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center; margin-bottom: 20px;">
                [Diagram Placeholder for Figure 12]
            </div>
            <p class="figure-caption">Figure 12. Fast arc fault detection and elimination in a single-busbar switchgear system provided with a tie-breaker and fed by two power transformers.</p>
        </div>
        <p>The arc fault protection system of Figure 12 incorporates arc detection and elimination hardware. In the REA arc fault protection system and an UFES have been combined. The switchgear system of the figure can, for example, be located in a large city.</p>
        <p>The arc fault protection system is based on two <span data-field="module_id_1">{{ module_id_1 }}</span> modules, which are interconnected using fiber-optic links. The CURRENT messages are sent between the main modules via fiber-optic links. The system is extended with <span data-field="module_id_2">{{ module_id_2 }}</span> modules for enhancing the selectivity of the protection system. The <span data-field="module_id_1">{{ module_id_1 }}</span> modules are provided with loop-type fiber sensors for detecting arc faults on the bus duct between the power transformers and switchgear systems. The fault current is measured with current transformers located on the HV side of the power transformers.</p>
        <p>The HV side of the power transformers providing a reach of the protection up to the terminals of the power transformer. The protection of both sections of the busbar system is accomplished with loop-type fiber sensors connected to two <span data-field="module_id_2">{{ module_id_2 }}</span> modules. In addition, each outgoing feeder is equipped with <span data-field="module_id_1">{{ module_id_1 }}</span> modules, which deliver selective arc protection in case an arc fault occurs in feeder-cable termination compartments.</p>
        <p>On detection of an arc fault, the <span data-field="module_id_1">{{ module_id_1 }}</span> module sends a TRIP signal to the Quick Release Units (QRU100) controlling the Primary Switching Elements (PSE), which make a rapid metallic three-phase earthing, this leading to an immediate extinction of the arc. To back up a fast isolation of the fault, the QRU100 trip is routed further on to the circuit breaker of the HV side of the power transformer.</p>
    </div>


